ngredient/brine":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/brine","n":"Brine","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Brine (or briny water) is a high-concentration solution of salt (typically sodium chloride or calcium chloride) in water. In diverse contexts, brine may refer to the salt solutions ranging from about 3.5% (a typical concentration of seawater, on the lower end of that of solutions used for brining foods) up to about 26% (a typical saturated solution, depending on temperature). Brine forms naturally due to evaporation of ground saline water but it is also generated in the mining of sodium chloride. Brine is used for food processing and cooking (pickling and brining), for de-icing of roads and other structures, and in a number of technological processes. It is also a by-product of many industrial processes, such as desalination, so it requires wastewater treatment for proper disposal or further utilization (fresh water recovery)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-brine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"]]],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"]]],["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine",[["Doenjang","dish/doenjang"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Rassolnik","dish/rassolnik"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Meju","ingredient/meju"],["Soybean","ingredient/soybean"],["Curing (food preservation)","ingredient/curing-food-preservation"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Hard-boiled egg","ingredient/hard-boiled-egg"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"],["Kidney","ingredient/kidney"],["Kidneys as food","ingredient/kidneys-as-food"],["Liver as food","ingredient/liver-as-food"],["Pearl barley","ingredient/pearl-barley"],["Pickled cucumber","ingredient/pickled-cucumber"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/98/Water_salinity_diagram.png/500px-Water_salinity_diagram.png","w":500,"h":701,"by":"Peter Summerlin","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Water_salinity_diagram.png"}},"dish/hamantash":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/hamantash","n":"Hamantash","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Jewish cuisine","dot":"#a99fb5","intro":[["A hamantash is an Ashkenazi Jewish triangular filled-pocket biscuit associated with the Jewish holiday of Purim. The name refers to Haman, the villain in the Purim story. In Hebrew, hamantashen are also known as אוזני המן (oznei Haman), meaning \"Haman's ears\". \"Haman's ears\" also refers to a Sephardic Purim biscuit, \"Orejas de Haman\", thought to originate in Spain and Italy, that is made by frying twisted or rolled strips of dough."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-hamantash"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamantash"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Papaver","ingredient/papaver"],["Poppy seed","ingredient/poppy-seed"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Tori Avey: Jewish Recipes","ref":"site/toriavey-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"toriavey.com","note":"Tori Avey's Jewish recipe index, often paired with notes on each dish's history.","url":"https://toriavey.com/jewish-recipes/","suggested":true},{"name":"The Nosher (My Jewish Learning)","ref":"site/myjewishlearning-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"myjewishlearning.com","note":"Recipe section of the nonprofit My Jewish Learning, covering Ashkenazi, Sephardi and Mizrahi home cooking and holiday food.","url":"https://www.myjewishlearning.com/recipes/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Jewish cuisine","links